Trilobites had some of the earliest complex eyes we know of. Like an insect's eye, many trilobite eyes were made of lots of tiny lenses packed together. The lenses were clear crystals of calcite.

A crystal of calcite usually splits light into two images. Trilobite lenses were lined up in a special direction that avoided this, so they saw clearly. Some kinds had huge eyes that wrapped round the head. Others, living in deep, dark water, had no eyes at all. Compare that with how [[@eyes-see|your own eyes]] work with soft, living lenses.

{{story:eyes-see}}

When food is available all year, animals can specialise. Some eat only one kind of fruit or leaf. Many plants depend on one particular animal to carry their [[pollen|pollen]] or seeds. The [[@fig-wasps|fig wasp]] is a famous example: each kind of fig has its own kind of tiny wasp, and neither can survive without the other.

The cacao tree, which gives us [[@chocolate-tree|chocolate]], comes from the Amazon region and is pollinated mostly by tiny flies called midges. Partnerships like these make ecosystems rich, but also fragile. Lose one partner and the other may vanish too.

{{story:fig-wasps}}

Water is heavy and costly to launch. Sending a single litre to the space station costs thousands of dollars. So almost nothing is wasted.

The station's urine processor boils urine at low pressure, collects the steam and cleans it through filters and chemical steps. Sweat and the moisture in astronauts' breath are also caught from the air. In 2023, NASA announced that the system was recovering about 98% of all the water. What comes out is cleaner than most tap water on Earth. Astronauts like to joke that today's coffee is yesterday's coffee.

{{story:water-purify}}

Left in a cave with no clocks or windows, most people's inner clock runs slightly longer than 24 hours. That means it is easier to stretch your day than to shrink it.

Flying west, from Delhi to New York, lengthens your day. You stay up late and it feels almost natural. Flying east, from New York to Delhi, shortens your day by about ten hours. You have to fall asleep when your body thinks it is still afternoon. That is why many travellers find eastward trips harder to recover from.

{{story:sleep-why}}

The most famous research submersible is Alvin, run by the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution in the USA. It has carried scientists into the deep since 1964, and has been rebuilt many times.

In 1977, Alvin's scientists found [[@geysers-hot-springs|hot springs]] on the sea floor near the [[@galapagos|Galápagos Islands]]. Around them lived giant red-tipped tubeworms, big clams and crabs, in total darkness. Their [[foodchain|food chain]] starts with [[bacteria|bacteria]] that use chemicals from the vents instead of sunlight. Some scientists think life on Earth may even have begun in places like this. It also made them wonder about [[@alien-life|life on other worlds]], in oceans hidden under the ice of moons. In 1986, Alvin visited the wreck of the [[@titanic|Titanic]].

{{story:alien-life}}

Gaur eat grass, leaves, fruit and fresh bamboo shoots. Like cows, they chew the cud, bringing food back up to chew it again. They live in small herds, usually led by an older female, while big males often wander alone and join herds in the breeding season.

Only one [[predator|predator]] regularly tackles an adult gaur: the [[@tiger-stripes|tiger]]. Even then it is a big risk, since a gaur can charge and toss a tiger with its horns. Leopards and [[@wild-dhole|dholes]], India's wild dogs, sometimes take calves. When danger appears, the herd may bunch together, with calves in the middle.

{{story:bison-return}}

Different jars do different jobs. A small chutney jar has short blades close to the bottom, so a spoonful of coconut doesn't escape them. A big liquid jar has blades bent up and down, which throw liquid outwards and create a whirlpool, or [[vortex|vortex]]. The vortex pulls food from the top back down into the blades.

Many Indian jars have bumps or ribs on the inside wall. These break up the swirl so food doesn't just spin around in a ring, untouched. Dry grinding works best in short bursts, because hard spices like turmeric are tough on the motor and the blades.

{{story:spices}}

Before the 1850s, few people wanted to ride a lift. If the rope broke, you fell. Then Elisha Otis invented a safety brake. At a fair in New York in 1854, he stood on an open platform high above a crowd and had the rope cut. The platform dropped a few centimetres and stopped.

His trick was a spring that snapped teeth into the guide rails the instant the rope went slack. Modern lifts use a smarter version, but the idea is the same. Without it, [[@skyscrapers|skyscrapers]] would never have been built, because nobody would climb 50 floors of stairs.

{{story:space-elevator}}

1947 में, जिस साल भारत आज़ाद हुआ, हुसैन बंबई के युवा चित्रकारों के एक छोटे-से समूह से जुड़ गए। उन्होंने अपना नाम रखा प्रोग्रेसिव आर्टिस्ट्स ग्रुप। इसके सदस्यों में एफ़. एन. सूज़ा और [[@sh-raza|एस. एच. रज़ा]] भी थे। वे एक नए देश के लिए नई कला चाहते थे, जो न पुरानी भारतीय शैलियों की नकल हो, न आँख मूँदकर यूरोप के पीछे चले।

हुसैन ने यूरोप की [[@modern-art|आधुनिक कला]], खासकर पिकासो के टूटे-बिखरे आकारों, को भारतीय लोक कला, [[@sculpture|मंदिरों की मूर्तिकला]] और लघुचित्रों के साथ मिलाया। उनके लोगों के चेहरों पर अक्सर कोई बारीक नाक-नक्श होता ही नहीं। उनके शरीर, रंग और मुद्राएँ ही सब कुछ कह देते हैं।

{{story:bollywood}}

Korea's first-birthday party, the doljanchi, is a big event. In the past, many babies didn't live to see their first birthday, so reaching it was worth celebrating.

The highlight is doljabi. The baby, dressed in bright traditional clothes, sits before a tray of objects: a pencil, money, thread and more. Whatever the baby grabs first is said to show the future. A pencil means a scholar, thread means a long life. Modern families add a microphone or a football. Everyone laughs, and nobody takes it seriously.

{{story:puzzle-birthday}}

The first traffic light was set up in 1868 outside the Houses of Parliament in London, to help horse carriages and people crossing. It had red and green lamps lit by gas, and a police officer turned it by hand. A few weeks later it exploded, injuring the officer, and the idea was dropped for years.

Electric [[@traffic-lights|traffic lights]] appeared in American cities in the 1910s. In 1923, Garrett Morgan, a Black inventor from Cleveland, patented a signal with a third position that stopped traffic in all directions. Today's [[amber|amber]] light does a similar job, a short warning before the red.

{{story:traffic-lights}}

Knot theory grew from a wrong idea. In the 1860s, the Scottish scientist Lord Kelvin suggested that [[atom|atoms]] might be tiny knots in an invisible substance filling space. Different knots, he thought, could be different [[element|elements]].

His friend Peter Guthrie Tait began drawing tables of knots to test the idea. The atom theory turned out to be false. But Tait's tables started a new branch of maths. In the 1920s, the German mathematician Kurt Reidemeister showed that any wiggle of a knot can be broken into three simple kinds of move, which made knots much easier to study.

{{story:dna}}

In the 1750s, the Swiss mathematician Leonhard Euler noticed something. For a cube, take the corners and subtract the edges, then add the faces: 8 − 12 + 6 = 2. For a square pyramid: 5 − 8 + 5 = 2. For a prism: 6 − 9 + 5 = 2.

It works for every solid without holes through it, however lumpy or strange. A shape with a tunnel through it, like a picture frame or a bead, gives a different answer. Mathematicians use this to tell shapes apart in a field called topology.

{{story:bubbles-round}}

After the Second World War, many Asian countries had just become independent. Leaders wanted a sports event that would bring Asian nations together. Guru Dutt Sondhi, an Indian sports official, pushed hard for the idea. In March 1951, the first Asian Games opened in New Delhi, with around 11 countries taking part.

[[@delhi|Delhi]] hosted again in 1982. The city built new stadiums and flyovers, and the Games' cheerful mascot was a baby elephant called Appu. India also began colour television broadcasting that year, so families could watch events in colour. For many households, the 1982 Asiad was the first time they saw a colour [[@tv-india|TV]].

{{story:japan-culture}}

[[epic|महाकाव्य]] [[@mahabharata|महाभारत]] में गुरु द्रोण अपने शिष्यों की परीक्षा लेने के लिए एक पेड़ पर ऊँचाई पर लकड़ी की एक चिड़िया रखते हैं। हर राजकुमार के तीर चलाने से पहले द्रोण पूछते हैं कि उसे क्या दिख रहा है। कोई कहता है पेड़, डालियाँ और आसमान। किसी को चिड़िया दिखती है और बगल में खड़े भाई भी।

अर्जुन जवाब देते हैं कि उन्हें बस चिड़िया की आँख दिख रही है। द्रोण कहते हैं, तीर चलाओ, और तीर ठीक निशाने पर लगता है। भारत में यह कहानी सदियों से एकाग्रता के सबक के तौर पर सुनाई जाती रही है। आधुनिक विज्ञान इसमें बस एक बात जोड़ेगा: अर्जुन को भी आराम चाहिए था, और कोई भी बिना ब्रेक के घंटों तक ऐसा ध्यान नहीं टिका सकता।

{{story:mahabharata}}

कई नाख़ूनों की जड़ पर जो हल्के रंग का आधा चाँद दिखता है, उसे *ल्यूनुला* कहते हैं। यह मैट्रिक्स का अगला सिरा है, जो ऊपर से झलकता है।

छोटे सफ़ेद धब्बे आम तौर पर पुरानी, बेनुकसान चोटें होती हैं जो बढ़कर बाहर निकल रही होती हैं। डॉक्टर कभी-कभी नाख़ूनों में सेहत के सुराग पकड़ लेते हैं, जैसे अजीब रंग या उभरी धारियाँ। अगर कोई नाख़ून अचानक बहुत बदल जाए, तो किसी भरोसेमंद बड़े को दिखाना ठीक है।

{{story:rhino-kaziranga}}

Light enters through the lens and lands on the sensor, a chip covered with millions of tiny light-catching squares called photosites. Each one measures how much light hits it and turns that into a number. Those numbers become the [[@pixels|pixels]] of your photo.

The photosites can only measure brightness, not colour. So a grid of tiny red, green and blue filters sits on top, a pattern invented by Bryce Bayer at Kodak in the 1970s. There are twice as many green squares as red or blue, because human [[@eyes-see|eyes]] are most sensitive to green. Software then fills in the missing colours for each [[pixel|pixel]] by looking at its neighbours, using the way red, green and blue [[@colour-mix|light mixes]] to make every other colour.

{{story:eyes-see}}

In 1665 [[@black-death|a deadly plague]] swept through England, and Cambridge closed. Newton, then 22, went home to Woolsthorpe for about two years. Working alone, he made progress on calculus, studied light and began to think about [[gravity|gravity]].

Newton later said that seeing an apple fall in the orchard set him wondering. If gravity pulls an apple down, might it reach as far as the Moon? The tree is still alive in the garden at Woolsthorpe, now looked after by the National Trust.

{{story:colour-mix}}

In the early 1990s, workers at car factories in Japan were scanning many ordinary [[@barcodes|barcodes]] on boxes of parts, one after another. Barcodes can hold only about 20 characters, and they must be scanned in the right direction.

Masahiro Hara, an engineer at a company called Denso Wave, which made parts for Toyota, wanted something faster that held more. He has said the idea for the corner squares came while playing the board game Go, with its black and white stones on a grid. In 1994 his team released the QR code. QR stands for Quick Response.

{{story:barcodes}}

In the monsoon, people in Sohra live with damp clothes, mould on walls and roads that turn into streams. Many still use a traditional rain shield called a knup. Made of split [[@bamboo|bamboo]] woven into a curved frame and covered with leaves, it sits on the back and head like a turtle's shell. Your hands stay free for farming or carrying loads.

Rain often falls hardest at night. Mornings can be misty, with clouds drifting through houses and over the road. Some days you cannot see more than a few metres.

{{story:umbrellas}}

[[@tolkien|J. R. R. Tolkien]] was a professor who studied Old English and other ancient languages. As a hobby, he invented his own, including two Elvish languages. He wrote The Hobbit in 1937 and The Lord of the Rings in the 1950s. He said the stories grew partly to give his languages a world.

His Middle-earth had thousands of years of history, family trees, songs and detailed [[@maps-history|maps]]. Readers loved the feeling that the world went on past the edges of the page. His friend C. S. Lewis wrote the Narnia books, starting in 1950. The two belonged to a group called the Inklings, who read their writing aloud to each other.

{{story:tolkien}}
